 { This position was reached in the online game Carlsen,M - Theodorou,N (2023) . Can you find the move that Magnus played in order to put a stop to Black's plans? } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { A powerful example of prophylaxis, with White making their own pawn break in order to stop Black's threat of ...g4, which would have opened up lines against the White king. } ( 1. a3 $2 { Allowing the pawn break would be a big mistake. } g4 $1 2. hxg4 hxg4 { Black has opened up the h-file, which makes White's king a lot less safe. Importantly, Black can now activate their dark-squared bishop via the h6 square in the future. Finally, the move ...gxf3 may also be very strong. } ) 
hxg4 2. hxg4 $1 { Correct! With the pawn break stopped, White can use his better structure to press the opponent. } ( 2. fxg4 $6 { would throw away any advantage, with Black able to invade along the open file after } Bxe4 3. Qxe4 Qxe4 4. Rxe4 Rf3 $11 { Black's more active rooks offset White's superior structure. } ) 
Bd7 ( 2... Bxe4 { Capturing on e4 may have been best, but it's understandable that Black did not want to do this since then it is clear that White's dark-squared bishop is permanently superior to Black's. } 3. fxe4 Rf4 { This square (which we study as a 'power outpost' in the Outposts section) is very attractive for Black's rook, but in this particular case White is ready to evict it immediately. } 4. Bg3 $1 Rf7 5. Rf5 $16 { In the end it is White's power outpost on f5 that turns out to be much more effective. } ) 
3. Kg2 $16 { White consolidates the position and will perhaps look to use the h-file in the near future. White's minor pieces are much better than Black's, while the g5 pawn is an enduring weakness. The backward pawn on f3 is not a major concern as it is easily defended. } ( 3. Be3 $2 { Magnus blundered in the game, forgetting that here Black could have played 3...Bxg4! winning a pawn. } ) 

