 ( { Sometimes the opponent will threaten multiple pawn breaks at once. In this game, Magnus Carlsen had the White pieces against GM Bortnyk. He spots that Black has two pawn breaks ( } 1. -- a3 { and } c6 { ) and finds a way to stop both of them. } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nb5 $1 { The a3 square is now covered, while ...c6 would leave the d6 pawn too weak. } Ba6 $4 { Black tries to dislodge the knight, though this is a major blunder. } ( 1... c6 $2 2. dxc6 Bxc6 3. Nxd6 { with a winning position for White. } ) 
 ( 1... a3 $2 2. Nxa3 $18 { Black has lost a pawn without inflicting any damage to White's structure. } ) 
2. Nec3 $2 { It's curious how focused both players were on the positional aspects of the position, as Magnus also overlooks the problem of Black's trapped knight. } ( 2. Qd2 $1 { This is the big problem that both players overlooked. All of a sudden the knight on b4 is trapped. } Bxb5 3. cxb5 $18 { White will capture on b4 next. } ) 
a3 { Black plays the break anyway, but here it does not work. } 3. Nxa3 $18 { White is close to objectively winning since Black has no compensation for the pawn. So far we have looked at situations where the defending side was able to stop the pawn break by force. Let's now take a look at what you can do when it is not possible to directly prevent a pawn break. } 
