 { Black is unable to stop White's threatened f4-f5 pawn break under good circumstances. In cases like this, a helpful question to ask yourself is the following: 'Given that the pawn break will happen, where would I like my pieces to be when it happens?' } 1... { -KEY- } Rhf8 $1 { Black anticipates the pawn break, and gets ready to position their rooks optimally on f8 and e8 to deal with the opening up of lines as well as the weakening of Black's kingside structure. } ( 1... Rad8 $2 { This was played in the game. However, now after } 2. f5 $1 exf5 3. exf5 Rhf8 4. fxg6 fxg6 5. Nf4 $1 Bf7 6. Bd3 { White is winning, since there is no way for Black to defend the g6 pawn. } ) 
 ( 1... f5 $6 { Technically, Black could have stopped the pawn break. However, this would allow White to consider e4-e5 in the future, and would also leave Black's g6 and e6 pawns chronically weak. } ) 
2. f5 exf5 3. exf5 Rae8 $1 4. fxg6 { White seeks out a similar plan to what we saw against 1...Rad8. However, this time Black is much better prepared. } fxg6 5. Nf4 { Black appears to be about to lose the pawn on g6, but because of their clever piece positioning, they are ready to meet White's tactical threats. } Rxf4 $1 { Eliminating White's powerful knight. The pressure against the e2 bishop ensures that this is not an exchange sacrifice but instead a fair trade of one minor piece for another. } 6. Bxc4 ( 6. Rxf4 $2 { loses after } Rxe2 $19 { Black is up material and has the more active pieces. } ) 
Qc7 $15 { Black has organised their forces well, and if either side is better it is Black. After all, White's position is not without its defects. Notice the weakness of the h4 pawn and a possible outpost on g4 for Black's knight to seek out. } 
