 { In the last example, we saw how Black positioned their pieces as well as possible to prepare for the inevitable pawn break. I should have followed a similar strategy in the game Timoshenko,G - Astaneh Lopez, A (2012) . } 11... { -KEY- } N7b6 $1 { Black gains time against White's light-squared bishop. } ( 11... O-O $6 { This was my choice in the game. However, it's a poor decision as White is immediately able to break on the kingside. } 12. f5 $1 exf5 13. Nxd5 cxd5 14. Bxd5 $16 { White is dominating in the centre. His d-pawn has the potential to become a protected passed pawn, while Black's f5 pawn will likely fall in the next few moves. } ) 
12. Bb3 ( 12. Bxd5 $6 Nxd5 13. f5 exf5 14. Nxf5 Bxf5 15. Rxf5 Qd7 $11 { With the reduction of material and the loss of White's strong light-squared bishop, the pawn break ceases to have a devastating effect. Black will soon castle kingside to buffer the defense of the f7 pawn. Additionally, Black has enduring pressure against White's central d4-pawn. } ) 
Qd7 $1 { By organizing my pieces this way, I would have taken the sting out of the pawn break. } 13. Qf3 { White could renew the threat by bringing their queen to support the break. } O-O-O $5 { Even if Black is unable to stop the break, castling long will take away White's chance at a kingside attack. } 14. f5 Bxf5 15. Nxf5 exf5 16. Qxf5 Qxf5 $1 { The mass exchange of pieces leaves the pawn break without teeth. } 17. Rxf5 f6 $1 { Black plays a break of his own, undermining the White centre. } 18. Ne4 fxe5 19. Rxe5 Rhe8 $11 { Black has no problems here, and if White is not careful their isolated queen's pawn on d4 might become a target in the future. One final and much rarer method to defend against a pawn break is by taking the most aggressive path. Let's take a look at what we mean with the next example. } 
