 { Black has just played the move ...h7-h5. It turns out that the only good way for White to meet this move is through a break of their own! } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { A case of attack being the best form of defense! White responds to the pawn break with a break of their own, which turns out to be much more powerful than the opponent's. } ( 1. h3 $2 { White would give away all of their advantage by defending the pawn. In fact, this would take away their possibility to ever play the f4-f5 pawn break. Let's show why this is the case: } hxg4 2. hxg4 Qb6 3. Rab1 O-O-O $1 { Black's king is now safe and the moment has passed. White should play a move like b2-b3 here, as this pawn break is now the most attractive given that Black's king has parked itself on the queenside. If instead White insists on the f4-f5 pawn break, their position would immediately collapse: } 4. f5 $2 gxf5 5. gxf5 Bxe5 $19 { Black is up a pawn and White's king is unlikely to survive the attack. } ) 
 ( 1. g5 $2 { This move would be very anti-positional, as it leaves Black with a power outpost on f5. } Ne7 $15 { This is one possible continuation for Black. Next, they will pivot with their h4 knight to f5. The position is objectively around equal, but in practical terms it's hard to play for White as they lack a clear plan. } ) 
hxg4 2. fxe6 $1 { With the in-between capture, White splits the enemy pawn chain in two. } Nf3+ { Black may have been banking on this check, but White has a strong reply. } ( 2... fxe6 $2 3. Qxg4 $18 { Black's position collapses as e6 and g6 are too weak. Additionally, White is threatening the move Bd2-g5 next, winning a piece. } ) 
3. Rxf3 $1 ( 3. Kh1 $2 Rxh2 4. Qxh2 Nxh2 $19 { with a decisive material advantage. } ) 
gxf3 4. exf7+ $1 { An important in-between move completes the winning sequence for White. From here the Black king will be unable to find a safe home. } Kxf7 { After } ( 4... Kf8 5. Qxf3 $18 { Material is roughly level but White has too many other advantages like a safer king, space, and the bishop pair. } ) 
5. Qxf3+ Kg8 6. Rf1 $1 { Black has no good defense against the combined pressure on d5 and g6. } ( { But not } 6. Bxg6 $2 { , since after } Nxe5 $1 7. dxe5 Qb6 $13 { Black is back in the game. } ) 
Ne7 $2 { Black tries to defend the g6 pawn, but gets mated: } 7. Qf7+ Kh7 8. Rf3 Qc8 9. e6 $18 { There is nothing to do against the threat of Rh3#. In this and the previous example, it was clear that the defending side needed to capture in response to the pawn break. Sometimes however, the question of whether you should capture the enemy pawn or whether it is best to ignore it is a lot less certain. Let's take a look at a couple of examples of this next. } 
