 { Black has just played ...f5, forcing White to decide between capturing the pawn or ignoring it for now. } 1. { -KEY- } b4 $1 { The correct decision. White eyes up their own pawn break on the centre/queenside, which is where they have a large space advantage. A series of pawn exchanges on c5 would also open up the b-file, which is in White's favour as they already have a rook placed on b1. } ( 1. exf5 $2 { It would be a mistake to capture the pawn. In general, you will want to capture if you a) improve your piece activity and/or b) improve your structure. In this case, the capture would have the opposite effect on both. } Nxf5 { The knight is headed to the powerful d4 outpost. } ( 1. b4 gxf5 $5 { Black could instead have captured with the pawn, improving their structure. } ) 
2. Be4 Nd4 3. Qd1 Nc5 $11 { Black has at least equality due to their powerful central knights. } ) 
fxe4 ( 1... f4 $6 { The pawn advance is not scary as this would leave Black weak along the light squares. This would play into White's hands since they are the only side with the light-squared bishop. Besides this, White has the c4-c5 pawn break that is far more dangerous than Black's slow kingside attack. } ) 
2. Qg4 $1 { White ignores the pawn for now, preferring to wait and capture it with the knight on the next move. } Nf5 3. Nxe4 Nd4 { Black has maneuvered their knight to the powerful d4 outpost, but this is not too worrying since White can play around this piece. Meanwhile, Black's passive bishop on g7 is a serious long-term problem. } 4. Bd3 $16 { In the near future White will break on the queenside with either b4-b5 or c4-c5. We'll close out the section with one more example, where you will have to decide for yourself how to meet the pawn break! } 
