 ( { Black has once again played the ...f5 pawn break. In this case, do you think it's best to play } 1. exf5 { or to support the centre with } 1. N1d2 $2 ) 
1. { -KEY- } exf5 $1 { Correct! Here the situation is very different since White can target the light squares after a series of exchanges on f5. } ( 1. N1d2 $6 { Ignoring the pawn break here would not be a good idea. } f4 $1 2. c4 b4 $17 { White does not have much play anywhere on the board, and they are now lacking in space on the kingside. Unlike in the previous example where White had active possibilities on the queenside, here there is no such counterplay. To make matters worse, Black's light-squared bishop will assist an eventual pawn break on g4. } ) 
Bxf5 2. Bxf5 $1 { Once the light-squared bishops come off, Black is left horribly imbalanced along the light squares. } Rxf5 3. Qd3 $1 Rf7 4. c4 $1 { Stabilising the centre, and challenging Black's remaining light-squared pawn. } ( 4. Qxb5 $6 Nxd5 $13 { is a lot less clear. } ) 
b4 { Black keeps the b-file shut down. } 5. Ng3 $16 { White will likely play Bc1-e3 next, perhaps followed by Nf3-d2-e4. After this, they can contest the a-file with Rb1-a1. We can see how in this example, the pawn break proved disastrous for Black after the series of exchanges on f5. Your judgment of pawn breaks will improve as you develop your overall positional understanding, since each situation must be considered in the context of the various positional elements at play. So far in the chapter we have focused on pawn breaks, either as the attacking side or the defender. In the two sections that follow, we'll take a look at ways in which you can use your pawns to target the opponent's structure and minor pieces. See you there. } 
